    "thm-emg-local-classical-charged-scalar-development": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-emg-trivial-potential-charged-scalar-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The associated **classical charged-scalar Einstein\u2013Maxwell system** is",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.2",
            "6.1",
            "7.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For finite $m\\ge1$, open $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, compact $K\\subseteq O$, $R\\ge0$, and integer $s\\ge5$, define the bounded admissible initial-state class",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Coefficients.** A **periodic symmetric hyperbolic coefficient family** on\n$O$ consists of smooth maps\n$$A^\\mu:I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O\\to\\operatorname{Sym}_m(\\mathbb R)\\quad(\\mu=0,1,2,3),\\qquad B:I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^m,$$\nsuch that $A^0(t,x,u)$ is positive definite for every\n$(t,x,u)\\in I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O$; here\n$\\operatorname{Sym}_m(\\mathbb R)$ is the real vector space of symmetric\n$m\\times m$ matrices, identified with symmetric endomorphisms of $\\mathbb R^m$\nthrough the standard coordinate inner product, and positive definiteness means\n$z^TA^0(t,x,u)z>0$ for every nonzero $z\\in\\mathbb R^m$\n([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $A^\\mu,B$ ($\\mu=0,1,2,3$) be a periodic symmetric-hyperbolic coefficient family on $I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O$ in the exact sense of [[def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system]]: the coefficient maps are smooth, each $A^\\mu$ is a real symmetric matrix, and $A^0$ is positive definite. Let $K\\subseteq\\operatorname{int}K'\\subseteq O$ be compact and let $R\\ge0$. Then there are $T>0$ and $C<\\infty$, uniform over all $u_0\\in\\mathcal S_s(K,R)$ of [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]], such that the system",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then each entry of this first metric jet has the regularity required by the first-order state class: the metric values are $H^{s+1}$ and the prescribed first derivatives are $H^s$. The harmonic-gauge vector $H^a=g^{bc}(\\Gamma(g)^a{}_{bc}-\\Gamma(\\bar g)^a{}_{bc})$ of [[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]] vanishes on the initial slice. The background $\\gamma$ is required to be smooth; choosing $\\gamma=h$ is not asserted to be valid for finite-regularity $h$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "lem-emg-fieldstrength-positive-symmetric-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The six evolution equations for $(e,b)$ have principal part\n$$n(e)-\\operatorname{curl}_h b=\\text{terms with no derivatives of }(e,b),\\qquad n(b)+\\operatorname{curl}_h e=\\text{terms with no derivatives of }(e,b).$$\nFor a spatial covector $\\xi$, let $C(\\xi)v:=\\xi^\\sharp\\times v$ in the oriented orthonormal frame. Set $\\theta^0:=\\alpha ds=-g(n,\\cdot)$ and decompose any spacetime covector as $\\zeta=\\zeta(n)\\theta^0+\\zeta_\\perp$, where $\\zeta_\\perp(n)=0$ and $|\\zeta_\\perp|_h$ is its spatial norm. The spatial principal block is the linear map on $\\mathbb R^3\\oplus\\mathbb R^3$ defined by $S(\\xi)(u,v)=(-C(\\xi)v,C(\\xi)u)$. It is real symmetric, has eigenvalues $0,0,|\\xi|,|\\xi|,-|\\xi|,-|\\xi|$, and satisfies $\\|S(\\xi)\\|=|\\xi|$. Therefore the principal symbol $P(\\zeta)=\\zeta(n)I_6+S(\\zeta_\\perp)$ is positive definite for every covector with $\\zeta(n)>|\\zeta_\\perp|_h$. In the adapted coordinates its time matrix is $\\alpha^{-1}I_6$, and every coordinate spatial matrix is symmetric. Derivatives of the frame and metric enter only as smooth lower-order coefficients depending on $(g,g^{-1},\\partial g,e,b)$; in a first-order metric state they are zero-order terms. The remaining two Maxwell equations are the constraints $\\operatorname{div}_h e=0$ and $\\operatorname{div}_h b=0$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "5.2",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "lem-emg-coupled-reduced-einstein-evolution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer and let $U=(g_{ab},p_{ab},q_{abi},e_i,b_i)$ be the finite-dimensional first-order state on the $2\\pi$-periodic three-torus, with $p_{ab}=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}$ and $q_{abi}=\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}$ for a fixed smooth background connection $\\bar\\nabla$. The component Sobolev spaces $H^s$ are those of [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]]. Assume an open admissible state set $O$ on which $g$ is Lorentzian, $g^{00}<0$, the inverse spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite, the lapse is positive, and the chosen state variables and coefficients are smooth. Let $K\\Subset\\operatorname{int}K'\\Subset O$ be compact sets and $R\\ge0$. Assume the draft local symmetric-hyperbolic supplier [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]] with its stated integer-$s$, coefficient, compact-state, choice and norm hypotheses. Then the harmonic-coordinate reduced Einstein equation with source $T_{ab}=\\mu_0^{-1}(F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\\tfrac14g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd})$, coupled to the six source-free Maxwell field-strength evolution equations, is a smooth quasilinear symmetric-hyperbolic system. For every initial state $U_0\\in H^s$ taking values in $K$ and satisfying $\\|U_0\\|_{H^s}\\le R$, its reduced system has a local solution with the regularity and compact-state bounds of [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]]. No Einstein or Maxwell constraint is assumed in this reduced-system statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "5.2",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L6",
          "source": "lem-emg-temporal-scalar-positive-symmetric-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "where $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ and $D_a\\pi^a:=g^{ab}D_a\\pi_b$. The temporal-gauge scalar first-order system is",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "5.2",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L7",
          "source": "lem-emg-charged-scalar-subsidiary-chain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then on a neighborhood of every initial point:",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "5.2",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L8",
          "source": "def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Gauge vector.** The **harmonic-gauge vector field** of the pair\n$(g,\\bar g)$ is the smooth vector field\n$$H^a:=g^{bc}\\bigl(\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\\bar\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}\\bigr) .$$\nThe difference of the connection coefficients is a $(1,2)$-tensor, so $H$ is a\nwell-defined smooth vector field independent of the chart used to express the\nsymbols ([[def-gr-tensor-fields-and-components]]).\n\n**(b) Reduced Einstein equation.** The **reduced Einstein equation** for the\npair $(g,T)$ with respect to $\\bar g$ is the second-order quasilinear system\n$$\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}-\\nabla_{(a}H_{b)}=\\kappa\\bigl(T_{ab}-\\tfrac12Tg_{ab}\\bigr)+\\Lambda g_{ab},$$\nwhere $\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}$ is the Ricci tensor of $g$\n([[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]]),\n$H_b=g_{bc}H^c$ and $\\nabla_{(a}H_{b)}=\\tfrac12(\\nabla_aH_b+\\nabla_bH_a)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "5.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L8",
          "source": "lem-gr-reduced-einstein-principal-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b) [first-order reduction]** Fix constants $\\kappa>0$, $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ and a prescribed smooth symmetric covariant $2$-tensor field $T$ on the chart domain. Fix a chart $(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ with\n$x^0=t$. Introduce the metric variables\n$$u:=\\bigl(g_{ab},\\ p_{ab}:=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab},\\ q_{abi}:=\\bar\\nabla_ig_{ab}\\bigr)$$\n(symmetry in $a,b$ understood) and the relation variables $\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}-p_{ab}=0$ and $\\bar\\nabla_0q_{abi}-\\bar\\nabla_ip_{ab}=\\mathcal C_{abi}$, where $\\mathcal C_{abi}=-\\bar R^c{}_{a0i}g_{cb}-\\bar R^c{}_{b0i}g_{ac}$. Second covariant derivatives include their derivative-slot connection. These known background-curvature terms are zeroth order in the first-order state and vanish for the flat coordinate background. Then the reduced Einstein equation can be solved for the second time derivatives in the form\n$$\\bar\\nabla_0p_{ab}=-(g^{00})^{-1}\\Bigl[2g^{0i}\\bar\\nabla_ip_{ab}+g^{ij}\\bar\\nabla_iq_{abj}+R_{ab}(x,u,T;\\kappa,\\Lambda,\\bar g)\\Bigr],$$\nwhere the source is smooth in the first-order metric state and the prescribed matter data, with no derivatives of $T$, and includes $2\\kappa(T_{ab}-\\tfrac12(g^{cd}T_{cd})g_{ab})+2\\Lambda g_{ab}$. The resulting first-order system for $u$ is symmetric hyperbolic on every\nregion where $g^{00}<0$ and the spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite:\nthe coefficient of $\\bar\\nabla_0u$ is the block diagonal\n$\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,g^{ij}I)$ (on the three slots\n$g,p,q$), which is positive definite there, and the coefficients of the spatial\nderivatives are symmetric. More generally, in a coupled reduction one may substitute a specified smooth algebraic stress map $T_{ab}=\\mathcal T_{ab}(x,u,z)$, symmetric in $a,b$, where $z$ is a specified additional first-order matter state ranging in an open Euclidean set. This keeps the same metric principal matrices; symmetric hyperbolicity of the combined metric-matter system requires a separate check of the matter equations.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "5.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L9",
          "source": "lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Let integers $s\\ge m\\ge0$ satisfy $s-m>3/2$, and\nlet $u\\in H^s(\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi};\\mathbb R^N)$ with the objects and notation of\n[[def-gr-periodic-torus-and-sobolev-spaces]]. Then:",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L9",
          "source": "lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b)** the pointwise product $uv$ of the continuous representatives lies in\n$H^k$ and\n$$\\|uv\\|_{H^k}\\le C_k\\,\\|u\\|_{H^k}\\,\\|v\\|_{H^k};$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L10",
          "source": "thm-gr-constraint-equations-in-normal-frame",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(c) Normal projections.** Define $(\\operatorname{div}_hK)(Y)=h^{ij}(D_{e_i}K)(e_j,Y)$, $\\tau=\\operatorname{tr}_hK=h^{ij}K_{ij}$ and $|K|_h^2=h^{ik}h^{jl}K_{ij}K_{kl}$. Then\n$$G(n,n)=\\tfrac12(S_h+\\tau^2-|K|_h^2),\\qquad G(n,Y)=(\\operatorname{div}_hK)(Y)-D_Y\\tau.$$",
          "uses": [
            "5.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L11",
          "source": "thm-gr-local-geometric-development",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then the data have a smooth time-oriented local development $(g,\\Psi)$ on an open variable-width neighborhood of $\\{0\\}\\times\\Sigma$ in the literal manifold $\\mathbb R\\times\\Sigma$, with induced data $(h,K,d)$, solving\n$$G(g)+\\Lambda g=\\kappa\\mathcal T(g,\\Psi),\\qquad\\mathcal E(g,\\Psi)=0.$$\nAfter restriction to a smaller collar, $\\Sigma$ lies in the interior of its intrinsic domain of dependence. The interior of that domain is a globally hyperbolic neighborhood with $\\Sigma$ Cauchy. Any two developments of this same model and these complete data are related near the entire initial surface by a data-fixing time-orientation-preserving isometry matching all matter fields. There is no uniform noncompact time width or global-in-time conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "5.2",
            "6.1",
            "7.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L9",
          "source": "lem-gr-smooth-composition-and-commutator-estimates",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, let $I$ be an open time interval, let $I_0\\subseteq I$ be a compact interval, and let $F:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^{m'}$ and $A:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\times O\\to M_{m'}(\\mathbb R)$ be smooth. Fix compact $K\\subseteq O$ and $R\\ge0$. Constants below depend on these data and are uniform in $t\\in I_0$. For $u,v\\in H^s$ with values in $K$ and norms at most $R$:",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L12",
          "source": "thm-gr-local-geometric-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Fix $\\kappa>0$, $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ and one covariant matter model: specified smooth tensor fields $\\Psi$, specified equations $\\mathcal E(g,\\Psi)=0$, and a specified stress expression $T=\\mathcal T(g,\\Psi)$. Covariance means pullback by a local diffeomorphism commutes with these equations and stress expression. Fix complete smooth matter Cauchy data $d$ for this model, a smooth Riemannian metric $h$ and smooth symmetric tensor $K$ on a smooth three-manifold $\\Sigma$, with $K(V,W)=g(\\nabla_Vn,W)$ for the future unit normal.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L13",
          "source": "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. If Omega has the specified finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation and $F\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathbb R^n)$, then $\\int_\\Omega\\operatorname{div}F=\\sum_j\\int_{S_j}F\\cdot\\nu_j\\,dS$, with faces counted once off E. All integrals are finite. In a specified finite gluing, the two fluxes on every shared face cancel.",
          "uses": [
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L13",
          "source": "thm-gronwall-integral-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $t_0\\le T$, let $u,a,b:[t_0,T]\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous, with $u,b\\ge0$, and suppose",
          "uses": [
            "7.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The prescribed normal/tangential two-form components determine the unique real two-form on $\\mathbb R n\\oplus T\\Sigma$: $F(n,\\mathfrak e_i)=-e_i$ and $F(\\mathfrak e_i,\\mathfrak e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$. Exactness $F_\\Sigma|_{T\\Sigma}=d_\\Sigma A_\\Sigma$ and $\\pi_i=\\mathcal D_i\\varphi_0$, together with $\\pi_0=\\pi_n$ in the initial temporal gauge, make all field and potential entries compatible. The jet in [L4] makes the harmonic vector initially zero. After the fixed invertible rescalings, every component is real and belongs to the stipulated $H^s$ class; the compact range lies in $K_0\\Subset K_1\\Subset O$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "L4",
            "A1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "On $O$, the metric block has the positive time matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,(g^{ij})I)$ from [L5] and [L8]; the total stress is algebraic in $(g,F,\\varphi,\\pi)$, so the scalar contribution changes only zero-order terms. The Maxwell block has time matrix $\\alpha^{-1}I_6$ and symmetric spatial matrices by [L5]; the sourced current is algebraic in $(g,\\varphi,\\pi)$ and hence is lower order. The charged scalar block has time matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(-g^{00},(g^{ij}))$ on each real and imaginary part by [L6]. The $\\varphi$ and $A_i$ transport equations add identity time blocks and zero spatial blocks. These blocks have no off-diagonal derivative couplings, so their direct sum is symmetric with positive time matrix. All coefficient maps are smooth on $O$ by smooth inversion of $g$, smoothness of $V$, and the stipulated open range.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L2",
            "L5",
            "L6",
            "L8",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Apply [L3] to this verified coefficient family, the state $u_0\\in\\mathcal S_s(K_0,R)$, and compact sets $K_0\\Subset K_1\\Subset O$. The solver gives the common interval, values in $K_1$, $L^\\infty H^s\\cap C H^{s-1}$ state regularity, uniqueness and stability, and smooth persistence for smooth initial data. It asserts only a solution to the reduced first-order system at this step.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The reduced coefficient system writes $u_s=-(A^0)^{-1}(A^i\\partial_i u+B)$ on a compact admissible range. Since $u\\in C H^{s-1}$ and the coefficient maps are smooth, the algebra and composition estimates give $u_s\\in C H^{s-2}$; differentiating this expression once gives $u_{ss}\\in C H^{s-3}$. The metric derivative residual $q_i-\\bar\\nabla_i g$ satisfies the homogeneous background-commutator ODE and has zero initial value, independently of the matter constraints, so $q_i=\\bar\\nabla_i g$; also $p=\\bar\\nabla_0g$ by its evolution. Thus the same Fourier norm and time-derivative argument as for the electrovacuum block gives $g\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C H^s$ and $g\\in C^3$ as a spacetime metric at the base order $s=5$: third spatial derivatives come from two spatial derivatives of $q$, mixed third derivatives from spatial derivatives of $p_s$, and third time derivatives from $p_{ss}$, all continuous since $H^2\\hookrightarrow C^0$. The algebraic reconstruction of $F$ from $(g,e,b)$ and the scalar component equations likewise give $F,A,\\varphi,\\pi\\in C^2$ where needed. In particular the smooth coordinate time levels have a $C^1$ transverse normal, the normal residual-transport arguments are legitimate, and the subsidiary wave energy has Lipschitz principal coefficients and bounded lower-order coefficients. This verifies the finite-regularity applicability of [L7] before using its conclusion.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L5",
            "L6",
            "L7",
            "L9",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The initial metric, scalar, potential, and Maxwell compatibilities and all Gauss and Einstein constraints are exactly those of [L7]. Applying that subsidiary result recovers $p=\\bar\\nabla_0g$, $q_i=\\bar\\nabla_i g$, $\\pi=\\mathcal D\\varphi$, $F=dA$, both sourced Maxwell equations, total-stress conservation before Einstein recovery, and $H=0$. Therefore the reduced solution is a full solution of the mathematical system in [F1].",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L4",
            "L7",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Since $q_i=\\partial_i g$ up to the fixed smooth background connection, the periodic Fourier norm identity gives $\\|g\\|_{H^{s+1}}\\asymp\\|g\\|_{H^s}+\\sum_i\\|\\partial_i g\\|_{H^s}$; similarly $p=\\bar\\nabla_0g$ gives the stated time-derivative regularity. From $\\pi_i=\\mathcal D_i\\varphi$, one has $\\partial_i\\varphi=\\pi_i+i\\lambda A_i\\varphi$. The $H^s$ algebra estimate in [L9] makes $A_i\\varphi\\in L^\\infty H^s$, and the same estimate in $C H^{s-1}$ gives continuity of the spatial derivatives. The Fourier norm identity $\\|f\\|_{H^{r+1}}\\asymp\\|f\\|_{H^r}+\\sum_i\\|\\partial_i f\\|_{H^r}$ then gives $\\varphi\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C H^s$. The field-strength, potential, and $\\pi$ regularities are the corresponding components of the reduced state. The embedding in [L9] supplies the classical derivatives needed by the equations at $s\\ge5$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L7",
            "L9",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-2",
          "step": "5.2",
          "claim": "For general smooth $\\Sigma$, regard $\\Psi=(A,\\operatorname{Re}\\varphi,\\operatorname{Im}\\varphi)$ as the fixed covariant matter fields; $F=dA$, $\\pi=\\mathcal D\\varphi$, $j$, and $T$ are the fixed equations and tensorial expressions of [F1]. On each relatively compact initial chart choose a flat comparison metric $\\bar g=-(dx^0)^2+\\delta_{ij}dx^i dx^j$, set $g_{ij}=h_{ij}$, $g_{00}=-1$, $g_{0i}=0$, $\\partial_0g_{ij}=2K_{ij}$, $\\partial_0g_{00}=-2\\tau$, and $\\partial_0g_{0i}=h_{ij}h^{k\\ell}\\Gamma(h)^j{}_{k\\ell}$. The Christoffel formula gives $H^0=\\tfrac12\\partial_0g_{00}+\\tau=0$ and $H^i=-h^{ij}\\partial_0g_{0j}+h^{k\\ell}\\Gamma(h)^i{}_{k\\ell}=0$ initially. Set $q_{abi}=\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}$, $p_{ab}=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}$, reconstruct $F$ from $(e,b)$, and use $\\pi_i=\\mathcal D_i\\varphi_0$, $\\pi_0=\\pi_n$, and $A_0=0$. Thus the complete data and all initial auxiliary constraints are specified; $A(n)=0$ fixes the initial temporal gauge. The equations and stress are covariant under local diffeomorphisms because $d$, the metric connection, contractions, and the oriented volume form commute with pullback. Steps 1.2 and 4.1 give the exact smooth symmetric-hyperbolic reduction, propagation of every auxiliary constraint, recovery of the matter equations, and $\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ before Einstein recovery. The initial constraints are precisely the normal projections in [L10]. These are the explicit reduction, initialization, conservation, and constraint hypotheses of [L11], so the local construction in that theorem gives a development near each initial point; the internal-gauge comparisons and collar patching are verified in the final comparison paragraphs below.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L5",
            "L6",
            "L7",
            "L8",
            "L10",
            "L11",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 4.1",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "(Fix both coordinate and internal gauges before comparison.) Given two smooth developments of the same complete data, use the wave-coordinate construction in [[thm-gr-local-geometric-uniqueness]] on each relatively compact initial ball: solve $\\Box_gx^\\mu=0$ with $x^0=0$, $x^i=y^i$, $n(x^0)=1$, and $n(x^i)=0$ on the initial surface. Its proof constructs these scalar waves by the same positive symmetric wave reduction and local solver used here, and their initial differential is invertible. The resulting charts are harmonic relative to the same flat comparison metric and have the identical initialized metric jet in step 5.2. A pulled-back potential need not satisfy $A'_0=0$. On each product collar set $\\chi(s,x)=-\\int_0^s A'_0(\\sigma,x)\\,d\\sigma$ and transform $A'\\mapsto A'+d\\chi$, $\\varphi'\\mapsto e^{i\\lambda\\chi}\\varphi'$. Then $A'_0+\\partial_s\\chi=0$; direct differentiation gives $D_{\\!A'+d\\chi}(e^{i\\lambda\\chi}\\varphi')=e^{i\\lambda\\chi}D_{\\!A'}\\varphi'$, while $F$, current and stress are unchanged. Since $\\chi|_\\Sigma=0$, tangential initial $A$, $\\varphi_0$ and the covariant normal derivative are unchanged. Only after this temporal-gauge restoration do both developments extract to the identical coefficient system of step 1.2 with identical complete initial values.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L5",
            "L6",
            "L11",
            "L12",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 5.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "step": "7.1",
          "claim": "(Local and whole-surface comparison.) On a smaller compact cylinder the two smooth states lie in a common convex admissible neighborhood. Subtract their equations; the coefficient mean-value formula puts their difference $v$ in $A^\\mu(u_1)\\partial_\\mu v+C v=0$, with bounded $C$. If $A^0\\ge aI$ and $b\\ge\\sup_{|\\xi|=1}\\|\\xi_iA^i\\|$, choose a shrinking ball with speed $\\sigma>b/a$. Symmetry and spatial integration by parts give $E'\\le CE$ for $E=\\int v^{\\mathsf T}A^0v$, since the side-flux matrix $\\sigma A^0+\\nu_iA^i$ is nonnegative. The zero initial difference gives $v=0$ in both time directions by Gronwall. This is the local coupled energy comparison in [L11], now applied after the internal gauge has actually been fixed. Undoing the gauges gives a local metric isometry and the stated matter-gauge equivalence. Future data-fixing metric isometries have unique germs: their differentials fix tangents and future normals, hence they agree along the normal geodesics from the initial patch. The injective variable-width normal collars and germ-patching construction of [[thm-gr-local-geometric-uniqueness]] therefore yield one metric isometry near the entire initial surface; no uniform width is imposed. For the existence/gluing argument of [L11], choose the common normal product collar and put each local potential into its temporal gauge there, with unchanged initial $A_\\Sigma$. On overlaps the local isometry comparisons just proved identify the metrics and $F$; two temporal-gauge potentials with the same initial spatial restriction and same $F$ agree, since their difference has zero time component, zero exterior derivative and zero initial pullback. On the common smooth normal-coordinate metric, their temporal-gauge scalar/potential blocks have the same coefficients and complete data, so the scalar-block version of the local energy comparison gives equality of the scalar representatives; no harmonic-coordinate equation for that common metric is needed for this matter-only comparison. Thus the local fields patch on the literal collar, as required for the smooth-development application in step 5.2.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L5",
            "L6",
            "L7",
            "L11",
            "L12",
            "L13",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 5.2",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-8-1",
          "step": "8.1",
          "claim": "(One global real gauge near the initial surface.) Pull the second development back by the metric isometry from step 7.1, and write $B=A_2-A_1$ on a common product collar. The local comparisons give $dB=F_2-F_1=0$, and the common initial data give $B|_{T\\Sigma}=0$. Define the global smooth real function $\\chi(s,x)=-\\int_0^s B_0(\\sigma,x)\\,d\\sigma$. Then $\\partial_s(B_i+\\partial_i\\chi)=\\partial_sB_i-\\partial_iB_0=0$ and $B_i+\\partial_i\\chi=0$ initially; also $B_0+\\partial_s\\chi=0$. Hence $A_2+d\\chi=A_1$ on the collar. The local scalar gauge comparisons have this same differential and zero initial value, so their functions agree with $\\chi$ and $e^{i\\lambda\\chi}\\varphi_2=\\varphi_1$. This gives the asserted global-potential gauge equivalence, with $\\chi|_\\Sigma=0$, without requiring a nontrivial bundle construction or assuming that a closed one-form on the entire spacetime is exact.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L11",
            "step 6.1",
            "step 7.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
